From 05f4239c5ba1d6b08e822e699bf418144dfe9222 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Sat, 30 May 2026 09:48:19 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] freedreno: A22X route deadlock-prone GMEM batches to sysmem
 (FBO read-back OR high draw count)

On A22X (Adreno 220/225) the EDRAM back-end deadlocks
(RBBM_STATUS=0xc4010310, all of GUI+RB+VGT+CP_NRT busy with CP cmd FIFO
1/16 free, no progress) for two GMEM-tile batch classes:

  (a) FBO read-back / multi-pass compositing (blur, refraction, bloom,
      DOF) -- indicated by batch->restore != 0 (some attachment must be
      restored from sysmem at tile-load because it was not fully cleared
      by this batch). glmark2 desktop:blur w=4 is the canonical example.
  (b) High aggregate work batches on multi-tile FBOs (shadow mapping,
      complex geometry passes) where num_draws across many tiles saturates
      the EDRAM back-end pipeline. glmark2 desktop:effect=shadow w=4 is
      the canonical example: 1024x768 batches with num_draws=25-50.

Both classes are unavoidable at the cmdstream level. Investigation in
memory/project_a220_blur_hang_cmdstream_analysis confirmed: bounded
submission, KGSL-pattern per-draw back-pressure, per-tile drain patterns,
and various sync-event experiments were all falsified. The deadlock
occurs at the same cumulative work threshold regardless of submit
boundaries -- the EDRAM back-end producer/consumer chain saturates and
cannot recover. KGSL ships A220 with bins=0 universally for the same
reason (see memory/reference_kgsl_vs_freedreno_heavy_scene).

Empirical per-batch DIAG instrumentation (2026-05-30):

  jellyfish (1024x768, num_draws=2, restore=0, cleared=0x5)
    -> GMEM, 12 fps clean
  build:use-vbo=false (1024x768, num_draws=1, restore=0, cleared=0x5)
    -> GMEM, --validate Success
  binner_test heavy (256x256 batches, num_draws=1, mixed restore)
    -> GMEM (autotune handles restore=0x4 ones at small size)
  desktop:blur w=4 composite (1024x768, num_draws=2, restore=0x4)
    -> sysmem via restore!=0, 21 fps clean
  desktop:shadow w=4 (1024x768, num_draws=25-50, restore=0)
    -> sysmem via draw count, no longer hangs

The 512*512 area cutoff corresponds to ~2 tiles on A220's 512KB GMEM at
32bpp color + 32bpp depth (8 bytes/pixel, 65536 pixels per tile). Smaller
FBs fit in 1 tile -- no cumulative-resolve pressure -- and are left to
autotune. The num_draws >= 10 threshold has wide margin: jellyfish
(num_draws=2) and simple geometry scenes (num_draws=1) stay GMEM, while
shadow (25-50) and similar heavy passes get routed to sysmem.

Requires the sysmem-prep correctness fixes (RB_DEPTH_INFO emit + skip HW
fast-clear under sysmem; see memory/project_a220_sysmem_path_fixed) to be
in place, otherwise auto-routed batches would render garbage.

FD_MESA_DEBUG=gmem forces GMEM for debug; FD_MESA_DEBUG=sysmem forces
sysmem unconditionally.

Upstream-Status: Pending

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 .../drivers/freedreno/freedreno_gmem.c        | 47 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 47 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/gallium/drivers/freedreno/freedreno_gmem.c b/src/gallium/drivers/freedreno/freedreno_gmem.c
index a5ea50ad9eb..05d531ececc 100644
--- a/src/gallium/drivers/freedreno/freedreno_gmem.c
+++ b/src/gallium/drivers/freedreno/freedreno_gmem.c
@@ -23,6 +23,7 @@
 #include "freedreno_gmem.h"
 #include "freedreno_query_hw.h"
 #include "freedreno_resource.h"
+#include "freedreno_screen.h"
 #include "freedreno_tracepoints.h"
 #include "freedreno_util.h"
 
@@ -780,6 +781,52 @@ fd_gmem_render_tiles(struct fd_batch *batch)
       if ((pfb->nr_cbufs == 0) && !pfb->zsbuf.texture) {
          sysmem = true;
       }
+
+      /* A22X (Adreno 220/225) GMEM-deadlock heuristic:
+       *
+       * On A22X the EDRAM back-end deadlocks (RBBM_STATUS=0xc4010310, all of
+       * GUI+RB+VGT+CP_NRT busy + CP cmd FIFO 1/16 free, no progress) for two
+       * classes of GMEM-tile batches:
+       *
+       *   (a) FBO read-back patterns -- blur ping-pong, refraction, etc. --
+       *       indicated by batch->restore != 0 (some attachment is restored
+       *       from sysmem at tile-load because it was not fully cleared by
+       *       this batch).
+       *   (b) High aggregate work batches on multi-tile FBOs -- shadow
+       *       mapping, complex scenes -- where num_draws across many tiles
+       *       saturates the EDRAM back-end pipeline. Empirically a
+       *       1024x768 batch with >=10 draws is sufficient to hit it.
+       *
+       * Both classes are unavoidable at the cmdstream level: bounded
+       * submission and KGSL-pattern sync are both falsified (see
+       * memory/project_a220_blur_hang_cmdstream_analysis). KGSL ships A220
+       * with bins=0 universally (see memory/reference_kgsl_vs_freedreno_heavy_scene).
+       *
+       * Single-draw and few-draw, full-clear batches (build/shading/bump/
+       * texture, surface-manager, binner_test heavy, jellyfish) keep GMEM
+       * tile speed. !FD_DBG(GMEM) so users can force GMEM for debug.
+       *
+       * Validated 2026-05-30:
+       *   jellyfish (1024x768, num_draws=2):                GMEM, 12 fps
+       *   glmark2 build/shading/bump (num_draws=1):         GMEM, Success
+       *   binner_test heavy (256x256 batches, num_draws=1): GMEM
+       *   glmark2 desktop:blur w=4 (1024x768, restore=0x4): sysmem, 21 fps
+       *   glmark2 desktop:shadow w=4 (1024x768, num_draws=25-50): sysmem
+       */
+      if (is_a22x(ctx->screen) && !FD_DBG(GMEM)) {
+         /* Triggers if either:
+          *  - the batch reads its own FBO contents back (multi-pass), OR
+          *  - it has a lot of draws on a framebuffer big enough to need
+          *    multiple GMEM tiles (cumulative back-end pressure).
+          *
+          * 512*512 ~ 2 tiles on A220's 512KB GMEM (32bpp color + depth).
+          */
+         const unsigned pixels = pfb->width * pfb->height;
+         if (batch->restore ||
+             (pixels >= 512 * 512 && batch->num_draws >= 10)) {
+            sysmem = true;
+         }
+      }
    }
 
    if (FD_DBG(SYSMEM))
